# The database state of mind

These six techniques are common to almost every great data story. Like any
other skill, they require practice, practice, practice.

<ul class="skills-grid">
  <li><Lightbulb aria-hidden="true" size={24} strokeWidth={2.25} /> Identifying measurable questions</li>
  <li><Database aria-hidden="true" size={24} strokeWidth={2.25} /> Locating data that can provide the answers</li>
  <li><Hammer aria-hidden="true" size={24} strokeWidth={2.25} /> Preparing raw data for rigorous analysis</li>
  <li><BarChart3 aria-hidden="true" size={24} strokeWidth={2.25} /> Interviewing prepared data to develop findings</li>
  <li><Map aria-hidden="true" size={24} strokeWidth={2.25} /> Testing your findings against the real world</li>
  <li><MessageCircle aria-hidden="true" size={24} strokeWidth={2.25} /> Ensuring your subjects have a say in the story</li>
</ul>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 1 of 6</p>

<div class="icon-heading">
  <Lightbulb aria-hidden="true" size={48} strokeWidth={1.75} />
  <h1>Identify measurable questions</h1>
</div>

Come up with questions that can be answered with measurements, numbers and
data — not just by talking to people.

<div class="step-example">
  <p class="step-example-label">Example</p>
  <p>During his campaign, Mayor Mamdani promised New York's buses would be
  faster and free. Both promises can be measured. Is the average bus trip
  faster than it was before he took office? Has the price of a bus ticket
  changed?</p>
</div>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 2 of 6</p>

<div class="icon-heading">
  <Database aria-hidden="true" size={48} strokeWidth={1.75} />
  <h1>Locate data that can provide the answers</h1>
</div>

It could be on a city data portal. It could require a Freedom of
Information Act request. It could require you to measure things yourself.

<div class="step-example">
  <p class="step-example-label">Example</p>
  <p>Say you're interested in measuring whether crime this year is up or
  down. You could download the individual crime reports dataset from the
  New York City data portal and analyze it by year.</p>
</div>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 3 of 6</p>

<div class="icon-heading">
  <Hammer aria-hidden="true" size={48} strokeWidth={1.75} />
  <h1>Prepare raw data for rigorous analysis</h1>
</div>

Typically, datasets require careful examination to understand what their
rows and columns contain. Unless you're very lucky, significant data
cleaning may be needed.

<div class="step-example">
  <p class="step-example-label">Example</p>
  <p>In the Federal Election Commission's database of campaign
  contributions, donors' names and occupations can be listed differently
  each time they write a check. Analyzing how much any one person has
  donated requires you to identify and combine all of the variations.</p>
</div>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 4 of 6</p>

<div class="icon-heading">
  <BarChart3 aria-hidden="true" size={48} strokeWidth={1.75} />
  <h1>Interview prepared data to develop findings</h1>
</div>

Treat data the same way you treat a human source. The only difference is
you can ask your questions using sorts, filters, groups and counts in a
data analysis tool.

<div class="step-example">
  <p class="step-example-label">Example</p>
  <p>If you wanted to ask the city's 311 complaints dataset what
  neighborhood had the most complaints this year, you would filter the
  database to the current year, group all of the individual records by
  their neighborhood column and then count how many records occur in each
  neighborhood. Finally, sort the result in descending order so the
  neighborhood with the most appears at the top.</p>
</div>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 5 of 6</p>

<div class="icon-heading">
  <Map aria-hidden="true" size={48} strokeWidth={1.75} />
  <h1>Test your findings against the real world</h1>
</div>

You should be able to follow your findings to the people and places they
describe. That's where you'll find out whether they're true, whether they
have any significance and whether there might be more to the story.

<div class="step-example">
  <p class="step-example-label">Example</p>
  <p>You could use the city's rental citations database to identify the
  buildings with the most outstanding violations. But you won't know
  whether those violations have been fixed, how much they truly matter to
  residents or what's causing them until you visit the buildings, see them
  with your own eyes and speak to the people involved.</p>
</div>

</Slide>

<Slide>

<p class="slide-eyebrow">Step 6 of 6</p>

<div class="icon-heading">
  <MessageCircle aria-hidden="true" size={48} strokeWidth={1.75} />
  <h1>Ensure subjects have a say in the story</h1>
</div>

You have an ethical obligation to allow the people you're writing about to
challenge your findings. Not only is it the right thing to do, but
listening to the people closest to your story can help make it better.

<div class="step-example">
  <p class="step-example-label">Example</p>
  <p>If you've done an analysis that shows a certain fire station has the
  slowest response times in the city, talking to the people who work there
  could help you better understand why that's happening. You owe them the
  opportunity to explain the data before you publish anything critical of
  their work.</p>
</div>

</Slide>

